#             Mastering Airflow: Backfill Strategies & DAG Lifecycle Management
A deep dive into historical data reprocessing, catchup mechanics, and the operational tools that keep your data pipelines reliable, consistent, and under control

#               MODULE 1 — Backfill Strategies: Reclaiming the Past

# Why Historical Data Processing is Necessary
      Data pipelines are NEVER perfect forever. Real production systems constantly change.

      Problems happen like:
        Code bugs
        Incorrect transformations
        Schema changes
        API failures
        Database outages
        Logic changes from business teams

      So historical data often becomes:
        Missing
        Incomplete
        Incorrect

#  Catchup vs Backfill

- Catchup - 
Catchup means:
  Airflow automatically creates all missed DAG runs.

  Example
  Suppose DAG schedule:
>    schedule="@daily"
>    catchup=True -- this is how we enable catchup

  DAG paused from:
  April 1 → April 10
  When you unpause on April 11:

  Airflow automatically creates:
    April 1 run
    April 2 run
    April 3 run
    ...
    April 10 run
  Automatically.

  Why?
  Because Airflow thinks:
  “These scheduled runs were missed. I should execute them.”

  Real-Time Example
  Suppose:
    Your company shuts down Airflow during maintenance for 5 days.
    When system comes back:
    You WANT all missed data processed automatically.
    Catchup is perfect here.

- Backfill -
Backfill means:
  Manually rerunning a specific historical date range.

  Operator decides:
    start date
    end date
    what exactly to rerun

  Example
    You discovered data issue only for:
      April 5
      April 6
      April 7
    You ONLY rerun these dates.
  That is Backfill.

# Catchup in Action
> catchup=True
is default Airflow behavior.

How Airflow Works Internally
When DAG unpauses:
Scheduler checks:
  start_date → current date
Then creates ALL missing intervals.

# Why Catchup Can Be Dangerous
If:
  > start_date=2020
  > catchup=True
and DAG was paused for years...

Airflow may generate THOUSANDS of runs.
This can:
  overload workers
  crash systems
  increase cloud cost
  overload databases
Very common production mistake.

# Backfill — Precise Historical Reprocessing
Backfill gives:
  surgical control
  targeted reruns
  no unnecessary executions

Real-Time Enterprise Example
Suppose:
Only March sales data corrupted
April and May are correct
With backfill:
>airflow dags backfill sales_dag \
>--start-date 2026-03-01 \
>--end-date 2026-03-31

Only March reruns.
Nothing else affected.

# Why Backfill is Safer
Unlike catchup:
  does not require DAG unpause
  does not trigger future runs
  isolates historical repair

<This is why enterprises heavily prefer backfill.>

# Automatic vs Deliberate Reprocessing
Catchup	                    Backfill
Broad	                      Precise
Automatic	                  Manual
Scheduler-driven	          Operator-driven
All missed intervals	      Exact selected range

Important Production Insight
Catchup = Scheduler Recovery
Backfill = Operational Repair Tool
Very important distinction.

# Backfill CLI Command
The primary way to trigger a backfill is through the Airflow command-line interface. The base command is straightforward, with powerful flags that give you fine-grained control over execution behavior.
> airflow dags backfill <dag_id> [OPTIONS]
Key Options:
  --start-date: The start date for the backfill (required).
  --end-date: The end date for the backfill (required).
  --ignore-first-depends-on-past: Ignores the first depends_on_past dependency.
  --reset-dagruns: Resets existing DAG runs in the specified date range.
  --rerun-failed-tasks: Only reruns tasks that previously failed.

example: 
The following command triggers a DAG run for every scheduled interval within April 2023. Airflow evaluates the DAG's schedule and creates one run per interval falling within the specified window.
  >airflow dags backfill my_daily_processing_dag \
  >  --start-date 2023-04-01 \
  >  --end-date 2023-04-30

What Happens Internally?
  If DAG schedule:
    @daily
  Airflow creates:
    30 DAG runs.
  Each run gets:
    unique logical date
    full task execution

