# Data Consistency Considerations

1. Idempotency - A task is idempotent if running it multiple times on the same input 
always produces the same output. T
  Document strongly emphasizes:
    Build idempotent pipelines.

  Best Practices
    Use UPSERT Instead of INSERT
    Wrong:
  >  INSERT INTO sales_summary
    Good:
  >  MERGE INTO sales_summary
    or:
  >  INSERT OVERWRITE

2. Partition by Logical Date
  Example:
    /sales/date=2026-05-01/
  Each rerun overwrites only that partition.
  Very common in:
    Spark
    Databricks
    Hive
    Snowflake
  Since you work with Databricks and PySpark, this is exactly how enterprise ETL pipelines are designed.

3. Avoid Side Effects
      Do not:
      send emails repeatedly
      duplicate notifications
      trigger external payments
      during reruns.

# > depends_on_past=True

This creates sequential dependency between runs.
Example
  April 2 task only runs if:
  April 1 succeeded

Useful for:
  cumulative balances
  rolling averages
  inventory calculations

# Sequential Execution with depends_on_past
This forces order-sensitive processing.
Banking Example

  Suppose daily balance calculation:
  Today's Balance =
  Yesterday's Balance + Today's Transactions
  You CANNOT process:
  April 10
  before
  April 9
  because data depends on previous day.

So:
depends_on_past=True
ensures:
  Apr 1 success
  then Apr 2
  then Apr 3
strict order.

Tradeoff
Advantage:
  consistency
Disadvantage:
  slower backfill
  Because parallel execution impossible.

# keep_dag_paused (Airflow 3.2+)
This is a newer operational improvement.

Old Problem
  Before Airflow 3.2:
  To backfill: DAG often needed unpause
  Problem: Scheduler also started: regular scheduled runs
  Result:
    accidental executions
    noisy logs
    resource waste

New Solution
  keep_dag_paused
  allows:
    historical reruns
    while DAG remains paused
  This is MUCH cleaner operationally.

# Operational Impact:  Concurrency & Resource  Management

Resource Planning is Essential - 
  Backfills can be extremely resource-intensive. Processing months of historical data simultaneously can overwhelm your Airflow workers, database connections, and downstream systems. Always plan your backfill window and concurrency limits carefully before execution
max_active_runs for Backfills
  Controls how many DAG runs within the backfill can execute concurrently. This setting is applied independently of the DAG's own max_active_runs configuration, giving you fine-grained control without modifying DAG code.
  Example:
    max_active_runs=3
    Only:
    3 runs execute together Others wait.

# Dry Run & Debugging
This is a VERY important operational safety mechanism.

--dry-run
Example:
  airflow dags backfill sales_dag \
  --start-date 2026-01-01 \
  --end-date 2026-12-31 \
  --dry-run

What It Does
  Shows:
    what WOULD run without actually running anything.
  Why Important?
    Prevents catastrophic mistakes.

Real Production Disaster Example
  Suppose engineer accidentally types:
  --start-date 2016
  instead of:
  2026
  Without dry run:
    thousands of DAG runs triggered
    systems overloaded
  With dry run:
    mistake caught safely

#                         MODULE 2 - DAG Lifecycle Management:  Beyond Backfills
Effective data engineering requires more than just reprocessing data. Managing a DAG through its full lifecycle — from active scheduling to pausing, versioning, and decommissioning — is what separates robust production pipelines from fragile ones

# DAG Lifecycle Management
A DAG has lifecycle stages:
  Stage	          Meaning
  Development	    Building DAG
  Testing	        QA/UAT
  Production	    Active scheduling
  Maintenance	    Paused/debugging
  Version         Upgrade	Logic changes
  Retirement	    DAG decommission

# DAG Pausing and Unpausing
Pausing a DAG
  When paused: Scheduler stops creating NEW runs.
  BUT: already running tasks continue
Unpausing
Scheduler resumes normal scheduling.

If:
  catchup=True
  then all missed runs immediately queue.
  Very important behavior.

Pausing & Unpausing in UI
  Airflow UI provides:
    toggle buttons
    DAG control page
Also CLI:
    >airflow dags pause <dag_id>
    >airflow dags unpause <dag_id>

#  DAG Versioning and Code Changes
DAG code is not static. As business logic evolves, tasks are added or removed, and schemas change. Each modification carries implications for both active scheduled runs and any planned backfills
- Impact on Historical Runs:
    Adding or removing tasks from a DAG mid-lifecycle can cause inconsistencies between old and new runs. A Important Airflow Reality
      Backfill always uses:
      Current DAG Code
      NOT old historical code.
      This surprises many beginners.

- Version Control is Non Negotiable
  Always manage DAG code in a version control system like Git. 
  Tag releases and use branches to test changes before merging to production. This provides an audit trail and enables rollbacks if a deployment causes issues

- Backfill Strategy for Code Changes
  When backfilling after a logic change, consider whether the new DAG version is backward-compatible. If not, you may need to run the old version of the DAG for historical ranges and the new version only for future runs.

